// mgeHost64 — SKY-directional ambient receiver (SH1, tasks/lighting.md).
//
// Ambient on every lit path was one flat colour: `a = gFrameData.ambCol.rgb` (or lodSunAmb on the
// distant paths). An upward-facing roof and a downward-facing arch soffit therefore received exactly
// the same ambient, and the only thing separating them was screen-space GTAO — which measures local
// occlusion and has no idea which way the sky is. So undersides, north faces and overhangs all read
// like roofs.
//
// The sky, meanwhile, is already an analytic function of direction. C2 moved the dome's colour out of
// the mesh and into sky.frag's VColSource == 3 branch, which is literally
//     lerp(fogColNear, skyZenith, saturate(dir.z))
// — a light source we were not sampling. The host projects that same function (plus a sun lobe, so
// ambient arrives warm from the sun's SIDE without touching the direct N.L term) into an SH-L1 basis
// once per frame and publishes it into gShadowParams. Evaluating it here is three dot products.
// Building the SH from the SAME dome function the sky draws is the point: the ambient cannot
// disagree with the sky the player is looking at, and it tracks weather with no reload.
//
// THIS ENHANCES, IT DOES NOT REPLACE. The published coefficients are normalised by their own DC, so
// what comes back is a directional FACTOR whose spherical average is 1. Callers keep their existing
// ambient colour and multiply:
//     a = gFrameData.ambCol.rgb * skyAmbFactor(N, worldPosRel);
// At strength 0 that is bit-identical to today's flat ambient; at 1 it is full directionality at the
// SAME overall brightness. The slider can only redistribute ambient, never dim or brighten the scene
// — which is exactly what makes it a safe A/B, and why the host normalises rather than publishing a
// replacement for ambCol.
//
// Pass the GEOMETRIC normal. opaque/alpha/multimap swap N for the GTAO BENT normal when that toggle
// is on; bending toward the unoccluded direction and then sampling directional sky would count the
// same occlusion twice.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SH2 — and how much of that sky can this point actually SEE (tasks/lighting.md).
//
// The SH above answers "which way is the sky brightest" while assuming the whole hemisphere is
// visible. It has no idea a wall is next to you, that you are in a canyon, or under an arch.
// Screen-space GTAO owns the fine end (contact shadows, a couple of hundred units) and only ever
// sees what is ON SCREEN. The band between GTAO's radius and the sky — buildings, cliffs, canyon
// walls, Red Mountain — was covered by nothing.
//
// gSkyHeight is a top-down max-height map of the world (terrain u statics) in a camera-following
// window, published through gShadowParams.skyAOMap. Marching a few taps outward and taking the
// steepest horizon gives that band directly:
//     vis = cos^2(horizon elevation)
// which is exactly the cosine-weighted fraction of a hemisphere left above a horizon at that
// elevation — so this is the physically-shaped term, not a curve that was tuned to look right.
//
// SEVERAL DIRECTIONS, NOT ONE MAX. Tracking a single max over a spiral makes any one tall occluder
// darken the point from every side, which is wrong in exactly the case this feature exists for
// (one wall of an alley, one canyon face). Independent horizons averaged cost the same taps and a
// few more registers, and a wall on one side then removes its own share of the sky rather than all
// of it.
//
// FIVE of them, an ODD count, and that is the whole reason for the number. Morrowind's world sits on
// an 8192-unit cell grid with its buildings square to it — a 4-fold symmetric world. Any EVEN set of
// azimuths is 2-fold symmetric (every direction has its own opposite in the set), so it shares that
// symmetry and aliases against it: the four walls of a courtyard get sampled by the same two axes
// twice. Five directions at 72 degrees have no opposite pair and no common factor with the grid's
// 4-fold symmetry at all, so no wall orientation is ever privileged.
//
// The taps START at an inner radius (default ~256 u) so this does NOT restate what GTAO already
// multiplied into the same ambient — the two are complementary bands, not stacked copies.
//
// Requires (include AFTER opaque.srt.h): gShadowParams.shAr/shAg/shAb + skyParams + skyAOMap,
// and gSkyHeight + gFrameData.lodEye.

#ifndef SKY_AMB_H
#define SKY_AMB_H

// The five azimuths, 72 degrees apart, phase-shifted 9 degrees off the axes:
//     9, 81, 153, 225, 297 degrees.
// 9 is not arbitrary. Five directions spaced 72 land on five DISTINCT offsets mod 90 (0,18,36,54,72
// shifted by the phase), so they cover a 72-degree spread inside the grid's 90-degree period and
// leave an 18-degree gap. Centring that gap on the axes — phase 9 — is what maximises the smallest
// angle any direction makes with a wall, at 9 degrees. Any other phase puts a tap closer to, or
// straight down, an axis-aligned wall.
//
// Written inline at the point of use rather than in a constant array: the project's other tap loops
// (msmrecv's PCSS disc) build their offsets the same way, and it keeps the samples as independent
// scalars instead of dynamically-indexed vector lanes, which is what lets them coissue.

// Sky visibility 0..1 at an ABSOLUTE world position. 1 = open sky.
// worldAbs.z is this point's own height, which is what every horizon is measured against.
float skyAOVisibility(float3 worldAbs)
{
    float4 m = gShadowParams.skyAOMap;   // xy = map origin (world), z = 1/extent, w = tap count

    // Map-space uv. Outside the window there is no data, and a clamp sampler would smear the edge
    // texel across the whole world beyond it — a wall at the boundary would shadow everything
    // behind it forever. Fade the term out over the outer eighth instead, so distant land leaves
    // the effect smoothly rather than crossing a ring.
    float2 uv = (worldAbs.xy - m.xy) * m.z;
    float2 e  = abs(uv - 0.5f) * 2.0f;
    float  edge = saturate((1.0f - max(e.x, e.y)) * 8.0f);
    if (edge <= 0.0f) { return 1.0f; }

    float inner = gShadowParams.skyParams.z;
    float outer = gShadowParams.skyParams.w;
    int   steps = (int)max(m.w, 1.0f);

    // Geometric step spacing: the near taps decide almost all of the answer (a horizon at 300 units
    // subtends far more sky than one at 6000), so spend samples where the signal is. logRatio is
    // the same for every direction and every step, so it is hoisted out of the whole march.
    float logRatio = log2(max(outer / max(inner, 1.0f), 1.0f));
    float myH      = worldAbs.z;

    // One running horizon SLOPE per direction — sin(elevation), which is what the visibility term
    // squares. Four ride a float4 so those marches vectorise into the same instructions; the fifth
    // is a scalar alongside. An odd count cannot pack evenly into vector lanes, and that is a fair
    // trade for not sharing the world's symmetry (see the header note).
    float4 maxS  = f4(0.0f);
    float  maxS4 = 0.0f;

    for (int k = 0; k < steps; ++k)
    {
        float t = ((float)k + 1.0f) / (float)steps;
        float d = inner * exp2(t * logRatio);
        // The step is taken in MAP space, so the whole march is five adds off one scaled radius.
        float r = d * m.z;
        float2 o0 = float2( 0.98769f,  0.15643f) * r;   //   9 degrees
        float2 o1 = float2( 0.15643f,  0.98769f) * r;   //  81
        float2 o2 = float2(-0.89101f,  0.45399f) * r;   // 153
        float2 o3 = float2(-0.70711f, -0.70711f) * r;   // 225
        float2 o4 = float2( 0.45399f, -0.89101f) * r;   // 297

        // SampleLvl, not Sample: this header is included by statics.vert, and a vertex stage has no
        // derivatives to pick a mip from. The map has one mip anyway.
        float4 h;
        h.x = SampleLvlTex2D(gSkyHeight, gSamplerBilinearClamp, uv + o0, 0).r;
        h.y = SampleLvlTex2D(gSkyHeight, gSamplerBilinearClamp, uv + o1, 0).r;
        h.z = SampleLvlTex2D(gSkyHeight, gSamplerBilinearClamp, uv + o2, 0).r;
        h.w = SampleLvlTex2D(gSkyHeight, gSamplerBilinearClamp, uv + o3, 0).r;
        float h4 = SampleLvlTex2D(gSkyHeight, gSamplerBilinearClamp, uv + o4, 0).r;

        // sin(elevation) = dh / sqrt(dh^2 + d^2). An empty texel carries SKY_HEIGHT_NONE, so dh is
        // hugely negative there and the max() below discards it with no branch — the sentinel
        // disarms itself.
        float4 dh  = h - f4(myH);
        float  dh4 = h4 - myH;
        maxS  = max(maxS,  dh * rsqrt(dh * dh + f4(d * d)));
        maxS4 = max(maxS4, dh4 * rsqrt(dh4 * dh4 + d * d));
    }

    float4 s   = saturate(maxS);
    float  s4  = saturate(maxS4);
    float4 vis = f4(1.0f) - s * s;          // cos^2(horizon) per direction
    float  vis4 = 1.0f - s4 * s4;
    float  ao  = (dot(vis, f4(1.0f)) + vis4) * 0.2f;   // average the five hemispherical wedges

    // strength lerps the whole thing back toward "fully open", so 0 is exactly the SH1 image.
    return lerp(1.0f, ao, gShadowParams.skyParams.y * edge);
}

// worldPosRel = the CAMERA-RELATIVE world position every one of the six lit paths already carries
// (near geometry is pre-shifted by -eye, distant statics are host-shifted, terrain subtracts
// lodEye in its VS). gFrameData.lodEye is the absolute eye and is written EVERY frame, interiors
// included, so this reconstruction is the same one water.frag uses for its world-anchored normals.
float3 skyAmbFactor(float3 N, float3 worldPosRel)
{
    // SH2 occlusion, on its OWN strength lane so the two halves A/B independently — SH off with AO
    // on is a legitimate thing to want to look at, and it would be unreachable if AO hung off the
    // SH's early-out. At AO strength 0 not one tap is taken and this whole block folds away, which
    // is the zero-change proof the feature ships behind. Interiors publish 0 in BOTH lanes (there
    // is no sky to project and no map to march), so neither needs a shader branch on interior-ness.
    float ao = (gShadowParams.skyParams.y > 0.0f) ? skyAOVisibility(worldPosRel + gFrameData.lodEye.xyz)
                                                  : 1.0f;

    // SH strength 0 = the directional half is off. AO still applies — it is a separate measurement.
    float s = gShadowParams.skyParams.x;
    if (s <= 0.0f) { return float3(ao, ao, ao); }

    float4 n = float4(normalize(N), 1.0f);
    float3 f = float3(dot(gShadowParams.shAr, n),
                      dot(gShadowParams.shAg, n),
                      dot(gShadowParams.shAb, n));
    // An L1 fit of a strongly directional field goes negative on the far side. Clamp rather than
    // rescale: rescaling would drag the whole sphere back toward flat, which is the one thing this
    // is for. The clamp only ever adds a hair of energy, so the "cannot darken the scene" property
    // survives it.
    // The AO scales the whole factor. SH1's "can only redistribute, never dim" guarantee is a
    // property of the SH ALONE and is deliberately not extended here — removing light is precisely
    // what an occlusion term is for. Keeping them as separate multiplies is what lets the panel
    // A/B either one against the other.
    return lerp(float3(1.0f, 1.0f, 1.0f), max(f, float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f)), s) * ao;
}

#endif // SKY_AMB_H
